      "title": "The real lock: requireAuth",
      "description": "**What it is:** the middleware that protects every logged-in API route. This is the lock that the browser-side login check (tour 02) is not.\n\n**How it works:** pull the token from the headers (`extractAccessToken`), then validate it with Neon Auth. No token or a bad token throws a **401**.\n\n**Why it matters:** even if someone skips the website and calls the API directly, they can't get in without a valid token.\n\n**Watch out:** this runs on every protected request, and for session tokens it means a database query every time (next steps)."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/middleware/auth.ts",
      "line": 99,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 75,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 99,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "Teams, and the request context",
      "description": "**How it works:** if the request names an organisation (`x-organization-id`), the code checks you're really a member of it. If not, it quietly falls back to your personal space. Then `c.set(\"user\", …)` stores your details for the route to read.\n\n**New idea — request context:** `c` is the request's context. `c.set` stores a value for this one request, and `c.get(\"user\")` reads it later, which is how the chat route knew who you were in tour 04.\n\n**Why it matters:** never trust an id the browser sends. Here a fake organisation id can't give you access to someone else's team.\n\n**Watch out:** the `email` and `name` aliases exist only so older billing code keeps working."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/lib/neon-auth.ts",
      "line": 207,
      "selection": {
