          "line": 207,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "Two kinds of token",
      "description": "**New idea — session token vs JWT:**\n- A **session token** is a random string. To check it, you look it up in the database.\n- A **JWT** (starts with `eyJ`) is a signed message containing your details. To check it, you verify the signature with the issuer's public keys (JWKS). No database needed.\n\n**How it works:** Neon Auth can hand out either, so this function looks at the first characters and picks the right check.\n\n**Why it matters:** JWT checks are faster. Session checks can be cancelled instantly by deleting the session.\n\n**Watch out:** session tokens carry no organisation, which is why the `x-organization-id` header (previous step) exists."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/lib/neon-auth.ts",
      "line": 150,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 134,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 150,
          "character": 14
        }
      },
      "title": "The session lookup",
      "description": "**How it works:** one SQL query joins Neon Auth's `session` and `user` tables, matches the token, and checks it hasn't expired (`expiresAt > NOW()`).\n\n**Why it matters:** this is where *who you are* is decided for most requests.\n\n**Watch out:** the tables live in a schema called `neon_auth`, managed by Neon. They're not in your Drizzle schema file, which is why this is raw SQL. It also logs the start of every token it checks."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/db/index.ts",
      "line": 28,
      "selection": {
