      },
      "title": "Protecting a whole route file",
      "description": "**How it works:** `chat.use(\"*\")` runs before every chat route. It applies `requireAuth`, except for addresses containing `/internal/`.\n\n**Why it matters:** one line protects every chat endpoint, so a new route can't forget the check.\n\n**Watch out:** the exception is a plain text check on the path. Internal routes must protect themselves (next step)."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/routes/chat.ts",
      "line": 44,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 14,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 44,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "The agent asks: is this token real?",
      "description": "**What it is:** the endpoint the Python agent calls before answering any message (tour 06).\n\n**How it works:** it requires the shared secret in the `X-Internal-Key` header. Then it validates the user's token with the same Neon Auth code and returns the user's id, email and organisation.\n\n**Why it matters:** the agent never touches Neon Auth directly. All login checking lives in one place.\n\n**Watch out:** this check is written safely: `!apiKey || apiKey !== expectedKey` fails if either is missing. Compare the next step."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/routes/providers.ts",
      "line": 1240,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1226,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1240,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "Handing the agent your Google token",
      "description": "**What it is:** the endpoint the agent calls to get your AdMob or Ad Manager access token, so its tools can act for you.\n\n**How it works:** after the key check, it finds your connected account (personal or team), respects your on/off preference, decrypts the tokens, and refreshes them with Google if they expire within 5 minutes.\n\n**Why it matters:** the agent never stores your Google tokens. It asks for them per question and caches them briefly.\n\n**Watch out:** the key check here is only `apiKey !== expectedKey`. If `INTERNAL_API_KEY` were ever missing on a server, a request with no key would pass, because `undefined !== undefined` is false. The chat route's version is safe. Also, the logs here print whether the keys match."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/server/routes/internal.ts",
      "line": 124,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 115,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 124,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "The internal routes' gate",
      "description": "**What it is:** the shared-secret check for `/api/internal/...`, like the entities endpoint the entity loader uses (tour 08).\n\n**How it works:** every request must carry the secret in `x-internal-api-key`, or it gets a 401.\n\n**Why it matters:** these routes return data for *any* user id, so the secret is all that protects them.\n\n**Watch out:** the same secret travels under **two** header names: `x-internal-api-key` here and `X-Internal-Key` for the token and validate-token routes. The Python side uses the right one for each, but it's an easy thing to break."
    },
    {
      "title": "What you can do now",
      "description": "### What you can do now\n\n- Explain Hono's building blocks: app, middleware, routes and sub-apps.\n- Walk through `requireAuth`, and explain session tokens vs JWTs.\n- Explain how team access is checked, and why the org header can't be trusted.\n- Describe how the agent talks to the API (shared secret, two header names) and the one place that check is weaker.\n\n**Next:** *06 - The agent server*. The Python server that receives your message and streams the answer."
    }
